{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"32c32f67f760c64194f6cd225774530145729599593ed3bf936abac545ce354a","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_49596aed6f8b372910ca52de97892cd77e69c2bcb5c7343fae26e2594ee9c378","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a methodologically sound extension of prediction difference analysis, introducing conditional and multivariate sampling to improve the fidelity of saliency maps. The experimental design is illustrative rather than comprehensive, relying on qualitative visual comparisons and a small medical dataset without quantitative validation metrics or statistical significance testing. Reproducibility is supported by detailed algorithmic descriptions and hyperparameters, though the absence of code and specific random seeds limits exact replication.","paper_id":"BJ5UeU9xx","paper_identity_sha256":"82472e2f4efc6825d4ce8a76b97e4ad366cf346d1cff15a1f265758b49b0b3c7","prompt_identity_sha256":"0409e99d1bc5e180cccf79b2be0ef2b26c9c9e6dd7f3473827f1ed820495f354","raw_response_sha256":"aac086195f8eec5802a4ef091bacda19f65ad1f88b4712fa42c3686c25a60d33","request_identity_sha256":"338cafdae4312844ef5e9f50e0ee3748750b837709b574ee4adaf247740f1e47","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"c071e1fec98a3a3d9bdca635e35adfca0f291ee0e34954f1cd6390ded4d5a3b7","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound, building on the probabilistic framework of prediction difference analysis. The authors correctly identify the limitations of the original univariate and marginal sampling approaches and introduce conditional sampling (Equation 4) and multivariate patch-based analysis to better model feature dependencies in images. The extension to hidden layers (Equation 5) is mathematically consistent with the input-output formulation. The algorithmic description in Algorithm 1 is clear and logically follows from the theoretical definitions, with no apparent conceptual errors in the derivation of the weight of evidence or activation difference metrics.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-026","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The experimental design is primarily qualitative, focusing on visual demonstrations of the method's output on ImageNet and MRI data. While the authors compare marginal versus conditional sampling and different window sizes, they do not provide quantitative metrics (e.g., correlation with ground truth saliency, classification accuracy impact) to objectively validate the improvements. The medical imaging experiment uses a small dataset (100 HIV, 70 healthy) and a simple logistic regression classifier, which limits the generalizability of the findings to deep learning applications in that domain. The lack of ablation studies on the specific choice of the multivariate normal distribution for conditional sampling, beyond a brief mention in future work, leaves the robustness of this design choice unverified.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproduction, including the full algorithm (Algorithm 1), specific hyperparameters for the experiments (e.g., patch sizes k=10, l=14 for ImageNet; k=3, l=7 for MRI), and the number of samples used (S=10). The authors specify the use of pre-trained models from Caffe and the specific datasets (ILSVRC, COBRA). However, the paper does not provide a link to the source code, nor does it specify the random seeds used for sampling, which could affect the exact reproducibility of the stochastic visualization results. The description of the MRI preprocessing pipeline is high-level, referring to in-house software, which may hinder independent replication of the medical imaging results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The paper positions its work relative to prior instance-specific visualization methods, such as class saliency (Simonyan et al., 2013), deconvolutional networks (Zeiler & Fergus, 2014), and layer-wise relevance propagation (Bach et al., 2015). It explicitly contrasts its probabilistic approach with gradient-based methods. However, the comparison is largely conceptual and qualitative; the paper does not include a direct side-by-side quantitative or visual comparison with these specific baselines on the same images to demonstrate the superiority of the proposed method. The comparison with logistic regression weights in the MRI section is appropriate for that specific domain but does not address how the method compares to other deep learning interpretability techniques in that context.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper lacks statistical rigor in its evaluation. The ImageNet experiments are illustrative, showing single instances without error bars, confidence intervals, or significance tests to support the claim that conditional sampling is superior to marginal sampling. The MRI experiment reports a single accuracy figure (69.3%) from 10-fold cross-validation but does not provide variance or standard deviation across folds, nor does it perform statistical tests to compare the visualization quality against the baseline (logistic regression weights). The reliance on visual inspection for the majority of the validation makes it difficult to assess the statistical significance of the observed improvements.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"b84412b3511ce9dfd1bb724232dcbb3abb882d4c75b9e0f44f2d036f5dbedbda","paper_id":"BJ5UeU9xx","paper_ordinal":20,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"aac086195f8eec5802a4ef091bacda19f65ad1f88b4712fa42c3686c25a60d33","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJ5UeU9xx","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"22546323ffdbd6374543db24488296dc9343b282c31f6cbc76d0d378fd24abbb","request_identity_sha256":"338cafdae4312844ef5e9f50e0ee3748750b837709b574ee4adaf247740f1e47","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1178,"prompt_tokens":18048,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19226},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"338cafdae4312844ef5e9f50e0ee3748750b837709b574ee4adaf247740f1e47","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}